Question 2
The 2021 WHO report states that the life expectancy of Bhutanese citizens has reached 74.9 years from 65.7 years in 2000.
According to the passage, what social and economic impact will Bhutan face with the increase in life expectancy? How can the government take responsibility to ease the burden of referral, and what duty can citizens take to help the nation reduce the same? (10 marks)', 'Social and economic impact: longer life is a success of Bhutan''s health system, but it means more elderly people. Older people suffer more from non-communicable diseases such as heart disease, diabetes, kidney disease, cancer and joint problems - the very conditions that make up most referrals (including hip and knee replacements). Health spending and referral costs will therefore keep rising; families will spend more time and money caring for elderly members; and with fewer working-age people for each dependant, there will be pressure on public finances and a need for long-term care and geriatric services.
Government responsibility: continue to shift budget towards prevention (from 15% to 30%, about Nu 5 billion in the 13th FYP); strengthen primary health care with regular screening and early treatment; train specialists and invest in equipment (as the Cath Lab and planned open-heart surgery team show) so treatment is available at home; and spread health information to the remotest areas.
Citizens'' duty: as the Director says, the government can only do so much. Citizens should eat a healthy diet with less salt, sugar and fat, avoid tobacco and limit alcohol, stay physically active, go for regular check-ups, follow treatment advice, drive safely, and use the health information provided to protect themselves and their families.', null, '[{"point": "Social and economic impact: more elderly people, more NCDs and long-term care, rising referral and health costs, pressure on families and caregivers, fewer workers per dependant, need for geriatric services", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Government responsibility: prevention budget (Nu 5 billion in 13th FYP), PHC, screening, specialists and equipment (Cath Lab, open-heart team), awareness, elderly care", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Citizens'' duty: healthy diet, less salt/ alcohol/ tobacco, exercise, regular check-ups, follow treatment, safe driving, use health information responsibly", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge') and t.slug = 'case-study-and-comprehension'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-2'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper II (General): BCSE 2024, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, General category, Paper II", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-general-2-2024b2-a', 2, null, null, null, null)
  returning id
