  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'Bhutan''s National Digital Identity (NDI) system recently received the "Special Award for Decentralised ID in practice" at the 2024 KuppingerCole European Identity and Cloud Awards in Berlin.
Discuss the socioeconomic benefits that integrating the NDI system with government, financial institutions, education and health systems will offer to the government and citizens. (10 marks)', 'Bhutan''s NDI is a decentralised (self-sovereign) digital identity: citizens hold their verified credentials - citizenship ID, certificates, licences - in a secure mobile wallet and choose what to share, instead of carrying paper documents. The international award shows Bhutan is a leader in this technology. Integrating it across sectors offers many benefits:
Government: services can be delivered online quickly and securely, reducing paperwork, queues, printing and staff time; fraud and fake documents are reduced because credentials are verified digitally; and accurate data helps planning and transparency.
Financial institutions: customers can open accounts and complete eKYC instantly, apply for loans and make digital payments, which lowers banks'' costs and brings rural and young people into the formal financial system.
Education: academic certificates and transcripts can be verified instantly for admissions, scholarships and job applications, ending fake certificates and repeated attestation.
Health: patients can share their health records securely with any hospital, avoiding repeated tests and speeding up treatment and referrals.
Citizens: save time, travel and cost, can access services from anywhere, and keep control and privacy over their personal data, while businesses can verify customers easily, supporting e-commerce.
To gain these benefits fully, the government must improve digital literacy, internet access in rural areas and cyber security.', null, '[{"point": "What NDI is: self-sovereign/ decentralised digital ID on mobile wallet, citizen controls own verified credentials", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Government: efficient online services, less paperwork and cost, reduced fraud/ identity theft, better data and planning, transparency", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Financial: easy eKYC account opening, digital payments and loans, financial inclusion of rural people", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Education and health: verified certificates, admissions and scholarships; health records shared securely, faster treatment", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Citizens: convenience, time and travel saved, privacy and control of data; plus a note on challenges (digital literacy, connectivity, cyber security)", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge') and t.slug = 'topical-issues-technology-and-governance'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-2'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper II (General): BCSE 2024, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, General category, Paper II", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
