  values ('ME', 'long_answer', '"In just over two weeks, more than 140 Bhutanese including those who have returned from abroad and those planning to return have registered with the National Reintegration Programme Desk." - BBS
What is your view on this programme initiated by the government? Will it affect the overall unemployment situation and force the government to create more job vacancies? Yes or No. Justify your answer. (10 marks)', 'My view: the National Reintegration Programme is a timely and positive initiative. Thousands of Bhutanese have gone abroad, mainly to Australia, causing a brain drain. The programme helps those who return to settle back by matching them with jobs, supporting them to start businesses, recognising the skills and qualifications they gained abroad and offering counselling. It shows the country values its people and wants their experience, savings and ideas to benefit Bhutan.
Will it increase unemployment and force the government to create more jobs? No, not seriously. Justification: (1) the number of returnees is small compared with those leaving, and many return with skills - nursing, hospitality, trades, IT - that are in short supply in Bhutan, so they fill existing gaps (for example, the shortage of nurses); (2) many bring savings and international work experience and can start their own businesses, which creates jobs for others; (3) the programme''s purpose is to match people to existing opportunities and private sector demand, not to create new civil service posts; (4) the government is already promoting the private sector and the Gelephu Mindfulness City, which will need workers. However, if large numbers return at once without matching skills, pressure on the job market will rise, so the programme must focus on skills matching, entrepreneurship support and private sector partnerships.', null, '[{"point": "View on the programme: positive - welcomes back skilled returnees, supports reintegration (jobs, business, skills matching, counselling), reverses brain drain, uses foreign experience and savings", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Clear Yes/ No stand with justification: e.g. No - returnees bring skills, capital and start businesses that create jobs; programme matches them with vacancies; or Yes - more job seekers in a small job market, pressure on government; balanced reasoning", "marks": 6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge') and t.slug = 'topical-issues-employment-and-migration'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-2'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper II (General): BCSE 2024, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, General category, Paper II", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
