  values ('ME', 'descriptive', 'Section 337 of the Road Safety and Transport Regulation 2021 states "A person shall stop a motor vehicle on the stop line to allow pedestrians to cross the road."
In your opinion, is it feasible to station a person (as stated above) on every stop line? Highlight the drawbacks of such a regulation. Elaborate how the safety of both road users can be ensured. Can there be better ways to cross the road? How? (10 marks)', 'The regulation actually requires drivers to stop at the stop line so pedestrians can cross; it does not require a person to be posted there. Stationing traffic police or attendants at every stop line would not be feasible - Bhutan has many crossings in towns and along highways, and it would need huge manpower and money.
Drawbacks of the regulation: it depends entirely on drivers'' discipline; enforcement is weak because police cannot watch every crossing; stop lines and zebra markings fade and are hard to see at night or in rain; pedestrians often cross anywhere rather than at crossings; drivers may not see pedestrians in time, especially on busy roads; and sudden stopping can cause rear-end collisions and traffic jams.
Ensuring safety of both road users: regular awareness for drivers and pedestrians (including in schools); strict enforcement with fines and CCTV; clear, well-maintained zebra crossings, stop lines, signs and street lighting; lower speed limits near schools, hospitals and markets; and pedestrians using crossings and looking both ways.
Better ways to cross: pedestrian-activated traffic lights at busy junctions; foot over-bridges or underpasses on highways and in Thimphu and Phuentsholing; raised crossings and speed humps that force vehicles to slow down; and crossing guards or volunteers near schools at peak hours only.', null, '[{"point": "Understands rule: drivers must stop at the stop line for pedestrians (not stationing a person); stationing staff at every crossing is not feasible - cost, manpower, many crossings", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Drawbacks: depends on drivers'' discipline, poor enforcement, unclear markings/ faded lines, pedestrians crossing anywhere, night/ bad weather visibility, traffic jams", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Ensuring safety: awareness for drivers and pedestrians, enforcement and fines, clear zebra crossings, signs, lighting, speed limits near schools/ towns", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Better ways: pedestrian traffic lights, foot over-bridges/ underpasses, raised crossings, speed humps, CCTV, crossing guards near schools at peak hours", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge') and t.slug = 'subjective-road-safety-and-regulation'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-2'), 'standard', 10,
