  values ('ME', 'descriptive', 'In 2023 alone, 259 nurses resigned, and currently Jigme Dorji Wangchuck National Referral Hospital (JDWNRH) has a shortage of over 380 nurses against the standard requirement for optimum service delivery.
Express how you foresee this shortage affecting the provision of services within the minimum required standard. What measures and interventions would you propose to ensure services are provided with minimum disruption? Submit two recommendations to fill the gaps so that services are provided as per health standards. (10 marks)', 'Effects of the shortage: with over 380 nurses short, the remaining nurses will carry a much heavier workload, leading to fatigue and burnout; patients will wait longer and receive less monitoring and care; the risk of errors and poor patient safety will rise; some wards, beds, operation theatres or specialist services may have to be reduced; and stressed nurses may also resign, making the shortage worse. Services may fall below the minimum standard of nurse-to-patient ratio.
Measures to reduce disruption: redeploy nurses from less busy units and district hospitals to critical areas; prioritise emergency, ICU, maternity and surgery; offer overtime pay and incentives; re-employ retired nurses and recruit on contract; shift non-nursing tasks (paperwork, transport, cleaning) to assistants and volunteers such as Desuups; and use technology to save nurses'' time.
Two recommendations to fill the gaps:
1. Retain nurses - improve pay and allowances, faster career progression, housing, manageable shifts, professional development opportunities and recognition, so fewer nurses leave for jobs abroad.
2. Train and recruit more - increase intake at the Faculty of Nursing and Public Health and private colleges with scholarships, provide bridging courses, and recruit qualified foreign nurses on short-term contracts until enough Bhutanese nurses are trained.', null, '[{"point": "Effects: heavy workload and burnout, longer waiting times, less patient care/ monitoring, more errors and risk to patient safety, closure or reduced services/ beds, more resignations (vicious cycle)", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Short-term measures: redeploy nurses, overtime/ incentives, recruit retired and contract nurses, task-shifting to assistants, prioritise critical units, volunteers/ Desuups for non-clinical tasks", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Two recommendations: retention (better pay, allowances, career progression, housing, flexible shifts, recognition) and training (increase nursing intake/ scholarships, faster training, bonding, recruit foreign nurses short-term)", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-general-knowledge'),
